        <td>Not captured <Unverified /></td>
        <td>Not captured <Unverified /></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="meta">Virtual Post Mail publishes plans on its pricing page, but its prices didn't load in our capture, so it isn't in the table yet.</p>

  <h2>What a month really costs: one example</h2>
  <p>Say you get 30 letters a month and want 10 of them opened and scanned (about 3 pages each), with nothing forwarded.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Traveling Mailbox Basic:</strong> $15. 30 envelopes and 30 page scans fit inside the 40-envelope / 35-page allowance <Src href={S.tmb} />.</li>
    <li><strong>PostScan Mail Starter:</strong> from $10, plus $2.00 for each of the 10 open-and-scans ($20), so from $30. The Starter plan lists no free open-and-scans; Standard includes 5 <Src href={S.psm} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Stable Grow:</strong> $59 monthly ($49 if paid yearly). 30 pieces is the monthly limit, and open-and-scan is free <Src href={S.stable} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Earth Class Mail Starter:</strong> $39 covers 5 digitized items, so 30 items is well past the allowance. The overage rate wasn't clear in our capture <Unverified />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Someone who scans very little and mostly wants an address does best with a cheap Anytime Mailbox or iPostal1 location. Anyone who wants most of their mail opened pays less with a plan that includes page scans.</p>

  <h2>Street address vs P.O. Box</h2>
  <p>Every provider above gives you a street address at a Commercial Mail Receiving Agency (CMRA), not a USPS P.O. Box. USPS requires every CMRA customer to sign Form 1583 before the CMRA can handle their mail <Src href={S.uspsBulletin} label="USPS" />. Some banks and agencies won't accept a CMRA address. Anytime Mailbox, for example, says it doesn't recommend using its address as your official business address because "some states and institutions may have restrictions" <Src href={S.atm} />.</p>

